infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Session resumption: where the state lives
  desc Both skip the costly asymmetric handshake for repeat clients — they differ on who stores the session secret
  compares
    - label Session IDs
      children
        - label Server stores the state
          desc Session secret kept in server memory, keyed by an ID
        - label Client presents an ID
          desc Returning client sends the ID; the server looks it up
        - label State is node-local
          desc Lives only on the server that issued it
        - label Needs shared storage to scale
          desc In a cluster another node lacks the ID — full handshake again
    - label Session tickets (RFC 5077)
      children
        - label Client stores the state
          desc Server encrypts the session secret into an opaque blob
        - label Client replays a ticket
          desc Returning client sends the ticket; the server decrypts it
        - label Server holds no state
          desc Any node with the ticket key can decrypt and resume
        - label Scales horizontally
          desc No shared cache; TLS 1.3 derives PSKs from tickets (0-RTT)
theme
  palette #6366f1 #14b8a6
